`walk_blocked_by: sink_before_source` means every sink call site precedes its
source in the same function, so the walk had no route to demonstrate. ADR-0017
§3a records the field and acts on nothing — ratified when the shape was 0.6% of
rows, and load-bearing once a primitive that is both source and sink raised it to
25% of the rows INV-lozat adds.

THE THREE PRECONDITIONS THIS ITEM SET, IN ORDER:

  1. The census. DONE: all 14 such rows on the 16-repository cohort read back
     against source — 1 true (7.1%), and BOTH verdict moves that rested on such
     a row alone were false.
  2. Can the walk distinguish "sink precedes source, no loop encloses either"
     from "inside a loop"? ANSWERED NEGATIVE, and this is the first time it has
     been established rather than assumed: the decision in `taint.py` is purely
     line-based (`sink_line > source_line`) and the DDG plumbing exposes no loop
     enclosure at that point. The narrow rule is not available today.
  3. Because (1) is not ~0, the remedy is verdict-NEUTRALITY, not removal.

So the rule is BROADER THAN IDEAL and says so in its own caveat text: it rests on
the census — where 0 of 14 rows had a loop enclosing both calls, so the narrow
rule would behave identically on the measured population — rather than on
per-row loop evidence.

A claim whose every surviving flow is unanimously blocked this way no longer
reads `violated`. The rows are STILL REPORTED: they ride as evidence, raise a
`sink_before_source_only` caveat, and add a clause to `details`, so the verdict
can never read as a plain `confirmed` on their account. Removal is what the
census did NOT license and is not what this does.

THE RULE READS `walk_blocked_by_values`, NEVER THE SCALAR. On a collapsed row the
scalar is `grp[0]`'s and says nothing about the other members — measured on
beads, 133 of 208 groups containing such a member are not unanimous. That field's
own docstring states the constraint; the mixed-group test enforces it.

Measured on the production path, against the pre-change arm of the same six-repo
cohort: gocryptfs `UI-FS` and caddy `UI-PROC` each move `violated` →
`inconclusive`, evidence count preserved at 1, reason disclosed. The behavioural
test carries its own control — the same primitives with the order swapped still
return `violated`, so a rule that merely broke the taint path could not pass it.

SEPARATELY, A PRE-EXISTING GAP THIS SURFACED: the coverage gate's `inconclusive`
downgrade dropped `caveats` entirely, so every structured disclosure vanished for
exactly the verdicts that are least certain, while the prose survived only
because `details` happens to be spliced. A consumer reading `caveats` saw an
empty list and could not tell an unqualified inconclusive from one carrying an
untyped-receiver or sink-before-source disclosure. They are carried through now.

The taint arm's declared-blindness branch had no live input on the shipped tree —
both language declarations went sighted with WI-nasuf and INV-misup — so rather
than pragma it, the test re-declares kotlin blind exactly as it stood on
2026-08-23, keeping INV-nuhun's both-arms rule pinned by a test instead of by the
accident of no language currently being blind.

